steps.body:
A set is iterable, so `for...of` visits each value in insertion order. `set.forEach((value) => ...)` does the same. Its callback gets the value twice, `(value, value, set)`, which keeps the same shape as a Map's `(value, key)` callback. It never gets an index, because a set has no positions. A string is iterable too, so `new Set("banana")` holds `b`, `a`, `n`.

```javascript
for (const letter of new Set("banana")) {
  console.log(letter);  // b, then a, then n
}
```

A set is not an array: `set[0]` is `undefined`, and it has no `length`, `sort`, or `join`. `set.values()` only gives an iterator. Turn the set into an array first with `[...set]` or `Array.from(set)`, then sort and join.

A **seen set** answers "have I met this before?" in one quick step. `seen.has(x)` stays fast however big the set grows, while `array.includes(x)` scans every item, which gets slow inside a loop.

Sets compare arrays and objects by reference, so `new Set([[1, 2], [1, 2]])` holds two entries and `set.has([1, 2])` is always `false`. Store a string key instead:

```javascript harness
const r = 1;
const c = 2;
{{code}}
